/*
	RMSTEST 4 — what height does a negative base_elevation actually produce?

	guide:957 says "Negative values maximally elevate a land (not recommended
	due to rendering issues)" and never says what "maximally" resolves to.
	guide:954 documents the range as 1-16, default 0. docs/preview-design.md
	Sec.6.1 was just changed to clamp negatives to 16 — that number is an
	INFERENCE from the word "maximally" and nothing else. It matters because
	Sec.4 declares elevation as a Uint8Array, so an unclamped -1 stores 255 and
	the renderer's brightness shading draws that land blinding white.

	This also incidentally tests guide:952's claim that an EMPTY
	<ELEVATION_GENERATION> section is enough for base_elevation to work at all.

	HOW IT DISCRIMINATES
	Three reference patches in a row across the middle of the map, on a flat
	grass base, each a different terrain so they are trivially told apart:
	  west   DIRT  base_elevation -1   <- the test
	  centre SNOW  base_elevation 7
	  east   DESERT base_elevation 16

	READ IT LIKE THIS
	  DIRT visibly the same height as DESERT -> maximum is 16. Sec.6.1's clamp
	                                            is right.
	  DIRT clearly higher than DESERT        -> the cap is above 16. Report how
	                                            it compares; Sec.6.1's clamp
	                                            value is wrong.
	  DIRT flat, level with the grass base   -> negatives are ignored or
	                                            treated as 0. Sec.6.1 is wrong
	                                            and should clamp to 0.
	  Nothing elevated anywhere              -> an empty ELEVATION_GENERATION
	                                            section is NOT sufficient;
	                                            guide:952 is wrong.

	Easiest way to judge height is the shadow/step count on the patch edges, or
	just rotate the camera low. Screenshot from a low angle if you can.

	RUN ON: Tiny, 2 players. Player lands are kept small and off-centre so they
	do not overlap the three patches.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain GRASS

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 5
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 42
}

create_land
{
	terrain_type DIRT
	land_position 25 50
	number_of_tiles 260
	base_size 5
	base_elevation -1
	zone 7
}

create_land
{
	terrain_type SNOW
	land_position 50 50
	number_of_tiles 260
	base_size 5
	base_elevation 7
	zone 8
}

create_land
{
	terrain_type DESERT
	land_position 75 50
	number_of_tiles 260
	base_size 5
	base_elevation 16
	zone 9
}

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>
